#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f429916c-f2e5-545e-baf3-3e4f2b8f4a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348447} {:answer/id #uuid "f890c957-ce64-57a6-b8dc-9900706f03dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f890c957-ce64-57a6-b8dc-9900706f03dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sec(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348448} :answer-field/id #uuid "7037f9c0-08ef-5855-ae15-a2d1779c3832" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1998fd88-1bb1-54f2-8d00-bb827ce3ebc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1998fd88-1bb1-54f2-8d00-bb827ce3ebc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348450} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25aa1f4-f9e1-553e-88f3-64ad734a5b87" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efde2ef3-da00-5f72-b88d-7d7aef4b94cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-325917" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/a1506b2272710ce21e0c58d327738dbe4afb3b134981a239e6e7882c3efb7072.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(4,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=4.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2170bc3f-f3e3-5eab-8d33-26d523add882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{81}-\\frac{y^2}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2170bc3f-f3e3-5eab-8d33-26d523add882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{81}-\\frac{y^2}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348453} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1181f40-9254-5b17-b74f-931115abd8cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c078f87-d5f5-5fde-be7c-c314f1607cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-325940" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d88f23e-d694-5796-8013-4a6b1b9b14eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d88f23e-d694-5796-8013-4a6b1b9b14eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348456} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4eb23f-7d8c-5a4f-a59e-5792b02417a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45c381d6-bc84-55d4-8f58-bec3fc4c32be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45c381d6-bc84-55d4-8f58-bec3fc4c32be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348458} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b7fb731-9191-519f-8736-5b7d21b6db36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5136f1b-23f4-5715-8858-5f369f611646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5136f1b-23f4-5715-8858-5f369f611646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348460} :answer-field/id #uuid "a739d859-bc53-59a2-8408-3a94e51820c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6212fbd3-023a-506d-a740-9d9958318e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325981" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/7facf793b5ce6740a31d665d96b03dd591e7edc2a6c35e519fa3b805534e02c1.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/80e49fe1187d1b9e42ef6e221568c9bafca1507238cc644afbda695f5cee2539.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }\\cup \\text{ }(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5ae8b95-379b-56c1-a212-cb30ba143de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348463} {:answer/id #uuid "d556ce73-f929-514c-b813-f21a1f57140c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348464} {:answer/id #uuid "8a78d314-f1a7-570d-a2b3-62de578a5537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348465} {:answer/id #uuid "df645401-4204-5051-bf82-f2ebec91fbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348466} {:answer/id #uuid "8f88b80d-eaf8-5c39-96e5-1040e1ddbac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f88b80d-eaf8-5c39-96e5-1040e1ddbac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348467} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b230544-3d90-5617-ba08-ba9583b22ef8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5237c29a-53d8-5c9a-b323-596badbbe2da" :question/math-academy-id "q-7988" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get]\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8c8c92a-aa84-5387-89cc-ffd443c248d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087283} {:answer/id #uuid "e5211b55-578f-5cde-873b-d12b5b12e6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (- π, 0), t \\ne - \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087284} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d0ffdc-4f9e-5bc4-858a-48c1aeb794b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [0, π], t \\ne \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087285} {:answer/id #uuid "50373bf6-f341-5011-817a-24c5a4de90d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})∪(\\frac{π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087286} {:answer/id #uuid "75564cf1-9da6-5201-b6e3-02ed66af31eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (0, π), t \\ne \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8c8c92a-aa84-5387-89cc-ffd443c248d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087283} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e17c5e-1f92-59fc-abf0-af1363d0c53b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9ce8075-169d-58ab-857b-a9a6feb55ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-89345" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/90b505954bb714ad86f60e07f7a26c16c954139689d96684ed553a7b494f0527.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/b43dcf6c343994e3226e2840a0ae80f60484c8de5d7984f61ec6447185181a76.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}]}
